Subject: compose: test the rule 0102 inverted, instead of the one it replaced

Four ApostateComposeTest cases fail against the applied series. The compile gate
cannot see them, because it compiles a translation unit without linking or
running one, so these were found by running base_unittests. Three are 0102's
blast radius and one is 0101's.

TheGpuClusterDoesNotFollowThePersona and
AnAnchorIsRequiredToBeServableByTheHostBackend assert, by name, the rule 0102
reversed: that --fingerprint-platform leaves the GPU cluster alone, and that an
anchor must match the host's graphics backend or composition declines.
docs/METHODOLOGY.md §9 already notes that patch commentary written against that
rule is still in the tree. These are the tests written against it, which is the
same problem in a stronger form: prose that contradicts the code is confusing,
a test that contradicts the code is a failing gate.

Both are rewritten rather than deleted, because the invariant they pin is still
wanted -- it inverted, it did not disappear. Deleting them would leave 0102's
central behaviour untested in the only place it can be tested without a browser.

  TheGpuClusterFollowsThePersona asserts a windows persona on a macOS host with
  a live Metal backend draws the Windows/D3D11 anchor, that the D3D11 cluster
  arrives with it rather than the id alone, that the §4 mismatch limitation does
  NOT fire because a drawn anchor always matches the persona, and that a macos
  persona on the same host draws the Metal anchor -- so the anchor is a function
  of the persona and not of the host or the seed.

  AnAnchorIsNotRequiredToMatchTheHostBackend asserts the D3D11 cluster is served
  over Metal, that it is served on a host whose backend is the software
  rasteriser -- the case that previously selected the software anchor -- and that
  the cross-backend limitation is still recorded, because the backend continues
  to decide which extension names the host's GL stack can back.

A third case is added, because 0102's most important rule had no test at all.
TheSoftwareAnchorIsNeverDrawn pins the exclusion across twenty-four seeds rather
than one, since it is a property of the candidate set and a single seed could
pass by luck, and it pins that --fingerprint-anchor still serves the software
anchor when it names it: un-defaulted, not removed. The fixture weights that
anchor 1000 against the others' 1, so a draw that considered it at all would
pick it.

WebGpuComesFromTheResolvedMemberNotTheAnchor was a stale fixture, not a stale
assertion, and its assertions are kept verbatim. The two members it needs -- one
that measured a WebGPU adapter and one that measured none -- hang off the
macOS/Metal anchor, and RequestFor() composes a windows persona, so after 0102
that anchor is no longer resolved and the gpu_identity axis is skipped
altogether. The request now names the macos persona. The behaviour it describes
is still correct and is the structural-coherence property worth keeping
prominent: WebGPU comes from the resolved member, which is the same member the
GL cluster comes from, so the two cannot disagree.

WorkAreaOriginFollowsThePanelOrigin is deleted. It sets screen.left=1920 and
expects avail_left==1920, but config/profile.schema.json declares no screen.left
and sets additionalProperties false under screen, so no admissible profile can
carry it -- which is exactly why 0101 removed the lookup. A test asserting an
input the schema rejects cannot pass, and re-pinning it to zero would pin the
behaviour of an impossible input instead. The coverage is retained: the sibling
case WorkAreaIsDerivedFromThePanelAndTheInsets exercises the same derivation on
an admissible input and passes.

The fixture gains two anchors, because the persona is now what selects among
them and one macOS anchor cannot serve three personas. Within a platform the
catalogue holds exactly one backend -- which is the property that let 0102 drop
the host-backend filter -- so each persona's candidate set here is a single
anchor and every draw below stays deterministic. RequestFor() also states its
persona explicitly with a comment, so a later change to DefaultPersonaForHost()
cannot silently move every test's anchor.

One test was encoding the old rule without naming it, which is why the sweep
mattered. AddingAnAxisDoesNotShiftAnotherAxisChoice compares
ChosenId(before, "anchor") with ChosenId(after, "anchor"), and between 0102 and
this patch both sides were "(absent)": the windows persona had no anchor to
resolve, so the line compared two absences and passed while testing nothing. The
new windows anchor restores it, and an EXPECT_NE against "(absent)" now stops it
going vacuous again quietly -- the same guard the case already had on its new
axis.

dispersion_table.h's comment on Anchor::backend said the field is "what host
servability is decided on -- not the platform persona", which is the withdrawn
rule stated in a header. Corrected to say what the field is still read for: the
"swiftshader" value that names the one anchor the draw must never choose, and
the limitation recording a cluster served over a backend that did not produce
it.

Run, not inferred. Before: base_unittests --gtest_filter='ApostateCompose*'
reported 26 tests with 4 failures, at compose_unittest.cc:562, :578, :594 and
:794. After: --gtest_filter='Apostate*' reports 33 tests, SUCCESS, all passed,
which is the compose suite plus the host-capability suite. The count is
unchanged at 26 for the compose suite because two were renamed, one added and
one deleted.

Ledger: webgl.unmasked-renderer, gpu.angle-backend, screen.availLeft.
